VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: SY4
ATTEMPT: 1
---
Scope per manifest: internal/services/metrics/metrics.go (+test),
internal/handlers/dashboard/handlers.go (+tests), internal/services/mcpsvc/spend/register.go +
summary.go (+test), internal/models/dashboard.go (gofmt-only touch), plus the sanctioned
mechanical `, nil` in internal/services/dataloader/transfers_test.go. Confirmed via
`git status --porcelain` + reading every diff hunk — no file outside this list is dirty beyond
the sanctioned deviations and pre-existing HC-merged/SY1-SY3-accepted state.

ATTACK 1 — Surfaces the enumeration might have missed. Independently grepped
`web/static/js` and both templates (kpis.html, dashboard-verdict-bar.html) for "living"/"Living"
and any client-side re-summing of outflows: no hits beyond what the manifest's table already
lists as CLEARED (rendering already-adjusted server fields). Independently confirmed
verdict.go (`BuildBudgetVerdict`) takes only `*models.DashboardMetrics` fields as input — no
local transaction arithmetic to re-classify. No missed surface found.

ATTACK 2 — HI + flagged overlap precedence, proven with a fixture rather than trusting the
test's name. Read TestCalculateMetrics_PlanExclusions_HIOverlapExcludedOnceNotDoubleSubtracted
in full: it builds a row that is BOTH Category="Health Insurance" AND present in
planExclusions, and asserts LivingExpensesTotal=3000 with an explicit comment that "a
double-subtraction bug would show 2300" — this is a genuine discriminating assertion, not a
name-trusting one, and PlanExcludedOutflows' HI-continue-before-map-lookup ordering (read
directly in metrics.go) matches it. Not a defect.

ATTACK 3 — Filtered-vs-unfiltered / twin-Hash leak. planExclusions is built by each of the
three call sites over the UNFILTERED active set (verified directly: handlers.go:126 and :217
pass `data.Active()`, handlers.go:284 passes `data.Active()` into the chart's planSyncExclusions
call, summary.go:246 passes `ts` — confirmed by reading the surrounding code that at that point
`ts` is `deps.load().Active()`, BEFORE `filtered := ts.FilterByDateRange(...)` a few lines
below). Re-verified (not re-assumed from the SY1 review) that
`internal/services/dataloader/loader.go:717-732` (`deduplicateTransactions`, unchanged, called
unconditionally inside every load) still keeps only the first transaction of any exact-Hash
collision, globally, before any range filter runs -- so a Hash claimed by the classifier always
corresponds to exactly one physical transaction with one fixed date; there is no "twin only
partly in range" scenario reachable through the real load pipeline. Not a defect.

ATTACK 4 — Comparison() reusing one map across both periods. Read metrics.go's diff: the SAME
`planExclusions` is threaded unmodified into both the current-window and comparison-window
`Calculate` calls. This is CORRECT, not a bug: a transaction's flagged classification is a
Hash-keyed fact about the ledger, independent of which window is being displayed (identical to
how coverageStart is a single lifetime fact reused across both windows) -- reusing one map is
the period-appropriate behavior here, not a shortcut that skips work.

ATTACK 5 — Cumulative-walk algebra cross-check between metrics.go and handlers.go (two
independent formulas for "actual spend minus plan-excluded" in the combined-cumulative walk:
metrics.go does `spend -= math.Abs(pe.SumAmount())` directly; handlers.go achieves the same
netting implicitly via `running += monthTarget - (livingMonth + hcAmt)` where livingMonth
already had excludedAmt subtracted). Verified algebraically these two paths compute the
identical mathematical quantity (`expAmt - excludedAmt`, hcAmt cancels). Float
non-associativity could in principle produce sub-cent divergence between the two paths, but
this is invisible at any rendered precision and the two figures are never displayed side by
side requiring bitwise equality — not a FAIL-worthy defect, noted only as a structural
observation.

ATTACK 6 — SIGN-CONVENTION DEFECT ON A NET-REFUND FLAGGED GROUP (CONFIRMED, FAILS AC5(a)).
`PlanExcludedOutflows` collects every matched row regardless of sign, and every consuming site
computes `excludedAmt`/`planExcludedTotal` as `math.Abs(planExcludedSet.SumAmount())` --
the ABSOLUTE VALUE of the flagged group's NET (signed) sum -- then SUBTRACTS that abs'd
quantity from an ALREADY-abs'd total (`totalExpenses`/`expAmt`, themselves
`math.Abs(outflows.SumAmount())`). This is only algebraically valid when the flagged group's
net and the remaining total's net have the SAME sign (the ordinary case: both represent real
spend). When a flagged group's refunds exceed its spend WITHIN THE WINDOW (net positive, "a
net refund" -- exactly the case SY1's ruling SY-2026-08-30a explicitly anticipated and
authorized for the sync-preview's ExcludedGroups display), the abs-then-subtract shortcut
produces the WRONG figure, because `|S + F| - |F| != |S|` when S and F have opposite signs
(only equal when same-signed, by the triangle-inequality boundary case).

PROOF (throwaway probe, added/run/deleted inside internal/services/metrics/, deletion
confirmed via `git status --porcelain` before writing this verdict; used the REAL
`metrics.Calculate`, not a reimplementation):
  Fixture: $8000 income; $3000 non-flagged rent (true remaining living spend); a flagged
  "Car Loan" group with one $2000 payment (Amount=-2000) and one $2500
  reversal/refund (Amount=+2500, same description, still typed Outflow) -- net for the
  flagged group = +500 (a net refund).
  Expected LivingExpensesTotal (the flagged group excluded entirely, whatever its own net):
  $3000 (just the rent).
  Actual result from the real Calculate():
    LivingExpensesTotal = 2000.00 (want 3000.00); PlanExcludedTotal = 500.00
    DEFECT: LivingExpensesTotal = 2000.00, want 3000.00
    TotalExpenses = 2500.00
  A $1000 error in the Monthly Living Expenses KPI -- money that should count fully as living
  spend (the $3000 rent) is silently under-reported by a third, purely because an unrelated
  flagged group happened to have a net refund in the same window. This directly contradicts
  AC5(a)'s literal requirement: "living actual drops by exactly the flagged net" -- here it
  drops by an amount that is neither the flagged net (500) nor zero, but a figure with no
  documented meaning (1000, which is exactly 2x the flagged group's abs'd net in this fixture --
  the generic error term is `|S+F| - (|S| - |F|)` whenever S,F have opposite signs).
  This same `math.Abs(...SumAmount())`-then-subtract pattern is used verbatim at EVERY
  consuming site (confirmed by direct read, not just this one probe): ActualMonthly/
  PerMonthDelta/CumulativeDelta (all derived from the same broken LivingExpensesTotal),
  LivingExpensesTrend's per-month bucket (`excludedAmt := math.Abs(pe.SumAmount())` inside
  Calculate's month loop), the combined-cumulative walk (`spend -= math.Abs(pe.SumAmount())`),
  handlers.go's buildBudgetVsActualChartData (identical per-month pattern), and MCP
  summarize_spending's living_monthly_actual/budget block (same Calculate call) -- so this is
  one root-cause defect that reaches every enumerated consumer, not an isolated corner case.
  None of the worker's 5 new metrics-package tests, 2 dashboard tests, or 1 spend-package test
  exercise a flagged group whose net is opposite-signed to the remainder -- every fixture uses
  single-direction (spend-only) flagged transactions.

Fix is confined to SY4's own territory (PlanExcludedOutflows/Calculate in metrics.go, and the
mirrored math in buildBudgetVsActualChartData): subtract the flagged group's SIGNED net from
the SIGNED remaining total before the final Abs, instead of subtracting two independently-abs'd
quantities -- e.g. compute the living figure as
`math.Abs(outflows-minus-HI-minus-flagged, signed sum)` in one pass, mirroring exactly how
`totalExpenses` itself is computed (one signed sum, one final Abs), rather than
abs-total-minus-abs-healthcare-minus-abs-flagged.

Build/test re-verification (own runs): `go test ./internal/services/metrics/...
./internal/handlers/dashboard/... ./internal/services/mcpsvc/spend/... -count=1` all `ok` (the
shipped suite is internally consistent -- it just never tests the sign-divergent case that
breaks the implementation).

STRONGEST ATTACK THAT FAILED TO BREAK IT: Attack 2 (HI+flagged overlap precedence) -- the
"double-subtraction" failure mode this run's rulings have repeatedly caught elsewhere is
correctly closed here, proven by a fixture with an explicit discriminating comment, not merely
a well-named test.

VERDICT: FAIL on Attack 6 -- a flagged group with a net refund exceeding its spend in the
displayed window produces a wrong, undocumented LivingExpensesTotal (and every derived
figure: ActualMonthly, PerMonthDelta, CumulativeDelta, LivingExpensesTrend, the
budget-vs-actual chart's Living bars/cumulative walk, and summarize_spending's
living_monthly_actual), violating AC5(a)'s literal requirement that living actual drop by
exactly the flagged net.
